 { Although the idea of the opposition is mainly seen in pawn endings, it occasionally turns up in other endgames as well, in particular when some piece is tied up to defensive tasks. Here are two examples. If we just consider his material advantage White should be easily winning; however, the active position of the black king and the reduced number of pawns makes the win rather difficult. } 1. { -KEY- } Ke5 $1 { Both sides are fighting for the 'side' opposition, and considering Black's spare tempo with the h-pawn, White places his king on a square of opposite colour to Black's. } ( { In contrast, the game went } 1. Ke6 $2 Kg5 ( { not } 1. Ke5 h5 $2 2. Kf6 ) 
2. Ke5 h5 $1 { and Black gained the opposition. After } 3. Ke6 ( { or } 3. Ke4 Kg4 $1 ) 
Kg6 $1 4. Ne3 { the players agreed a draw in view of } Kg5 5. Ke5 h4 6. g4 h3 { etc - Dvoretsky. } ) 
Kg5 ( { The alternatives are no better: a) } 1... h5 2. Kf6 { and the king enters. } ) 
 ( { b) } 1... h6 2. Kf6 $1 Kh5 3. Nxh6 $1 Kxh6 4. g4 { and wins. } ) 
 ( { c) } 1... Kf3 2. Ke6 $1 Kg4 3. Kf6 { (triangulation) } Kh5 4. Ne3 { with a barrier and White wins easily. } ) 
2. Ne3 { This is a so-called 'barrier', by which the knight and a pawn (or bishop) prevent the entrance of the enemy king. } h5 ( { Or } 2... h6 3. Ke4 h5 4. Kf3 h4 5. g4 { and wins. } ) 
3. Nf5 { Simplest, in order to regain the opposition. } ( { Also possible is } 3. Ke4 { approaching his own pawn, just like in the previous line: } h4 4. g4 h3 5. Kf3 Kh4 ( { or } 5. Nf5 h2 6. Kg2 ) 
6. Nf5 Kg5 7. Ng3 $1 ( { but not } 7. Kg3 $2 h2 ) 
h2 8. Ne4 Kh4 9. Nf2 Kg5 10. Kg3 { etc. } ) 
Kg4 ( { Or } 3... Kg6 4. Kf4 { with an elementary win. } ) 
4. Kf6 Kf3 5. Kg5 { White wins. } 
