 ( { On } 6... h6 { (here or later) White simply repeats the previous bishop manoeuvre: } 7. Be2 Bb7 8. Bf1 $1 Bc8 9. Bd3 { with the same scenario. } ) 
7. exd4+ Kd5 8. Bc4+ Kd6 ( { Or } 8... Ke4 9. d5 h4 10. gxh4 Kxf4 11. Kd4 $1 -- { followed by } 12. d6 { , and the black king proves fatally obstructed. } ) 
9. d5 ( { A slower plan with } 9. Be2 { was also possible, as noticed by Alejandro Botta. White takes the h5-pawn, then places the bishop on the long diagonal, followed by Kc3-c4 and d4-d5. After that, the king goes to d4 and the bishop to d3, with a decisive zugzwang. For instance, } Bb7 10. Bxh5 Bc6 11. Be2 Bb7 12. Bf1 Bc8 13. Bg2 h6 14. Kc4 Bd7 15. d5 Bb5 16. Kd4 Bd7 17. Bf1 Bc8 18. Bd3 { (zugzwang) } Bb7 19. Bxf5 Bxd5 20. Bc8 { , etc. } ) 
