 { Despite the reduced material White is in some practical trouble due to her king being cut off from the f-file. } 1. { -KEY- } Ke3 ( { Correctly avoiding the transposition to a pawn ending by } 1. Rf4 $2 { when } Rxf4 2. Kxf4 Kf6 $1 { leads to a decisive opposition. } ) 
 ( { Baburin also suggests } 1. Rg1 $1 { moving the rook to the edge, a sensible idea in the endgame and one that we will see more of in Chapter Three. } ) 
Rf1 2. Ke4 Kf6 3. Rg3 ( { The text doesn't spoil the draw, but } 3. Rf4 $1 { was possible now; i.e. } Rxf4 4. Kxf4 { and it is White who takes the opposition. Such a 'bridge' is a common feature in rook endings, and we will meet this subject inChapter Three as well. } ) 
g6 4. Ke3 Kf5 5. Ke2 ( { Again, } 5. Rf3 Rxf3 6. Kxf3 { was the simplest road to the draw - Baburin. } ) 
Rf4 6. Ke3 g5 7. Rg1 $2 { White's hesitance to enter a pawn ending finally dooms her. } ( { After } 7. Rf3 $1 { she would soon draw by opposition; e.g. } Kg4 ( { or } 7. Rg1 Rxf3 8. Kxf3 ) 
8. Rxf4 gxf4 9. Kf2 f3 10. Kf1 Kg3 11. Kg1 ) 
 ( { Even } 7. Rg2 $1 g4 8. Rf2 { in the same spirit was possible - Baburin. } ) 
g4 8. Ke2 ( { After } 8. Rg2 Rf3 9. Ke2 Kf4 { White lacks the space to save this rook ending; e.g. } 10. Ke1 ( { or } 10. Rg1 g3 { followed by ...Rf2 } ) 
 ( { or } 10. Rh2 Kg3 $1 { with ...Kg2 and ...g4-g3 next } ) 
g3 11. Ra2 Kg4 { and the black king will soon manage to assist the pawn. } ) 
Kg5 9. Rf1 g3 $1 10. Ra1 ( { Or } 10. Rxf4 Kxf4 11. Ke1 Ke3 $1 12. Kf1 Kf3 { etc - Baburin. } ) 
Kg4 11. Rh1 Re4+ ( { In view of } 11... Re4 12. Kd3 Re8 { or } 12. Kf1 Kf3 { White resigned. } ) 

